  "text": "To find the sum of the y-coordinates of the intersection points between the parabola \\( x = 34y^2 \\) and the piecewise linear function \\( f(x) \\), we need to determine the points of intersection by solving the equations simultaneously.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the piecewise function \\( f(x) \\) for clarity:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\\n2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3)\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe need to solve for the points where \\( x = 34y^2 \\) intersects with these pieces of the function.\n\n**Case 1: \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\)**\n\\[ 34y^2 = x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have \\( 34y^2 \\in [-1, 1) \\). This implies:\n\\[ -1 \\leq 34y^2 < 1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 < \\frac{1}{34} \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\leq y < \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\]\n\n**Case 2: \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\)**\n\\[ 34y^2 = 2 - x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have \\( 2 - x \\in [-1, 1) \\). This implies:\n\\[ -1 \\leq 2 - x < 1 \\]\n\\[ -3 \\leq -x < -1 \\]\n\\[ 1 < x \\leq 3 \\]\n\\[ 1 < 2 - x \\leq 3 \\]\n\\[ -1 \\leq x < 1 \\]\n\nHowever, this case is not valid since \\( x \\) cannot be both in \\([1, 3)\\) and in \\([-1, 1)\\) simultaneously.\n\nThus, the only valid case is \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\).\n\n**Sum of the y-coordinates:**\nThe y-coordinates for \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\) are \\( y \\in \\left[-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right) \\).\n\nThe sum of the y-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\sum_{y \\in \\left[-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right)} y = 0 \\]\nThis is because the function \\( f(x) \\) is symmetric around the y-axis, and the y-coordinates range from \\(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\) to \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\), which are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the y-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
